# Grant the bench user access to a Silicon Labs CP2112 HID USB-to-SMBus bridge.
#
# Unlike 63-benchctrl-adu218.rules, the route in here is /dev/hidraw*, not
# raw USBDEVFS. Two facts make that the right choice on this board, and both
# were measured rather than assumed:
#
#   1. `usbhid` DOES claim this device (1-1.2.3.2.3:1.0 appears under
#      /sys/bus/usb/drivers/usbhid/), so a hidraw node exists — hidraw2 on
#      this unit, named "Silicon Laboratories CP2112 HID USB-to-SMBus Bridge".
#      Contrast the ADU218, which usbhid deliberately ignores via
#      hid_ignore_list, leaving no node and forcing the usbfs path.
#
#   2. `hid-cp2112` is NOT built for this kernel (nothing matching *cp2112*
#      under /lib/modules). That matters: where that module IS present it
#      binds the chip and exposes it as a kernel i2c adapter instead, and
#      /dev/hidraw would then be contested by a driver that thinks it owns
#      the SMBus engine. Here nothing competes for it.
#
# The kernel creates hidraw nodes root:root 0600, so without this rule
# os.open("/dev/hidraw2", O_RDWR) fails with EACCES before any protocol work
# happens. The GPIO commands are HID **feature** reports (HIDIOCSFEATURE /
# HIDIOCGFEATURE ioctls), which need write access even for a get.
#
# Scoped to this one VID/PID rather than a blanket hidraw rule — that scoping
# is the whole point. A blanket `SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", MODE="0660"` would also
# expose the attached USB KEYBOARD (hidraw0/hidraw1 here) to the bench user,
# which is a keylogging surface, not a bench instrument. Do not widen this.
#
# 10c4 is Silicon Labs; ea90 is specifically the CP2112. Note 10c4:ea60 is the
# far more common CP210x UART bridge — a different chip with a different
# protocol that presents a tty, and it must not match this rule.
SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea90", \
    MODE="0660", GROUP="dialout"

# A stable symlink IS offered here, unlike for the ADU218.
#
# The reason is that hidrawN numbering is assigned in HID-enumeration order
# across every HID device on the system, so it moves when an unrelated
# keyboard is re-plugged — hidraw2 today was hidraw1 before the keyboard was
# attached. That is a worse race than the ADU218's, because the neighbouring
# nodes are input devices: opening the wrong one reads keystrokes.
#
# The CP2112 reports a real USB serial number (00EC63C9 on this unit), so the
# link is per-instrument rather than per-adapter and stays correct with two
# bridges attached. The driver still verifies the serial from the descriptor
# after opening; the symlink is a convenience, never the identity check.
SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea90", \
    SYMLINK+="benchctrl/cp2112-$attr{serial}"
